# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# next.js / production
/.next/
/out/
/build

# packaging output: the staged copy and the zip `package.sh` writes. Committed
# once by a stray `git add -A`, the next prep rsyncs a file it has just deleted
# and dies without saying why.
/dist

# testing
/coverage

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
next-env.d.ts
*.tsbuildinfo

# Dev scaffolding: the picks the switch writes on every click, and the contact
# sheet the reference draw is shown on. Both come off before the template ships,
# and neither is ever worth a commit.
.switch-picks.json
.refs-sheet.png

# The guides dragged out of the grid overlay's rulers, per route: one person's
# working marks, read by the agent from disk and never the project's.
.guides.json

# The requests written on the page with the picker, one block each, for the
# agent to process when told. Working notes: the commits that answer them are
# what the project keeps.
.comments.md

# Fontshare faces, under the ITF Free Font License: free to use, not free to
# hand on. Keeping the files out of git is what guarantees they can never
# travel in a sale zip — `npm run fonts` fetches them from fontshare.com, and
# public/fonts/README.md is the buyer's copy of the same instruction.
/public/fonts/*.woff2
/public/fonts/FFL.txt

# The reference library is shared between templates and lives outside every
# repo, so nothing about it is committed — not the images, and not the link.
#
# A symlink records an absolute path on one machine. Committed, it reaches every
# other machine pointing at nothing, and `next build` stops on
# `ENOENT: stat 'design-refs'` before it compiles a line — Vercel and a second
# laptop fail identically, while the machine that wrote the link sees nothing
# wrong. `stand-up.sh` writes the link when it stands a repo up, and README.md
# carries the one-line `ln -s` for a repo that already exists.
#
# Tailwind is held off the library by `@source not "design-refs"` in
# app/globals.css, which is the one place that job is done.
design-refs

# The audit skill's pictures and reports: one run deep, rewritten by the next,
# and evidence for the agent that fixes rather than any part of the product.
/.audit/
